\author{kellenkanarios}
\title{Page clustering}

\p{An amortization trick for the write side of [page replacement](HK73). Writing one dirty page to swap at a time pays a full disk I/O per eviction, and small random writes are the worst case for a disk. Page clustering batches them: the OS collects a group of dirty pages from the global dirty list and writes them out together in a single larger, more sequential I/O. The per-page cost of paging out falls because the fixed overhead of a disk write is spread across the whole batch.}
